"q-104140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+v+1)⟩"] ["math" "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})⟩"] ["math" "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1)⟩"] ["math" "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{e}^{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}⟩"] ["math" "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1)⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}y-{z}^{3}=3.$ \n What is the third component of $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-{z}^{3} & =3 \\\\ {z}^{3} & ={x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}y-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}y-3}\\,k.\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cffe361c135459c261777ef317ddae9665bbbd907f8873539f00567489831ce6"} {"question" "q-104181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt[3]{uv-3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3uv}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{u-3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}v-3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$2x-3y+\\sqrt[3]{2z}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+\\sqrt[3]{2z} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2z} & =3y-2x \\\\ z & =\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cffe361c135459c261777ef317ddae9665bbbd907f8873539f00567489831ce6"} {"question" "q-104182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-3v+2u)^{3}"] ["math" "(3v-2u)^{3}"] ["math" "2(-3v+2u)^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{(-3v+2u)^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2}=1$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1\\ge 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cffe361c135459c261777ef317ddae9665bbbd907f8873539f00567489831ce6"} {"question" "q-104170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt[3]{(u+v)^{2}-1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}-1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{(u+v)^{3}-1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{z} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cffe361c135459c261777ef317ddae9665bbbd907f8873539f00567489831ce6"} {"question" "q-104172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4})"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)"] ["math" "{e}^{{u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1}"] ["math" "{u}^{2}+{v}^{4}"] ["math" "{u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=2$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2\\ge 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cffe361c135459c261777ef317ddae9665bbbd907f8873539f00567489831ce6"} {"question" "q-103787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2-{u}^{3}-{v}^{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}}"] ["math" "{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}"] ["math" "{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the second component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cffe361c135459c261777ef317ddae9665bbbd907f8873539f00567489831ce6"} {"question" "q-103789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡r"] ["math" "\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡r"] ["math" "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}}={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta $\nfor $z\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={r}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta | \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$ Also, we drop the absolute value bars because $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\ge 0$ for  $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$  \n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is ${r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cffe361c135459c261777ef317ddae9665bbbd907f8873539f00567489831ce6"} {"question" "q-104273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization \n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-104278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cffe361c135459c261777ef317ddae9665bbbd907f8873539f00567489831ce6"} {"question" "q-104278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.j