256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cffe361c135459c261777ef317ddae9665bbbd907f8873539f00567489831ce6"} {"question" "q-103795" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103795" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "q-103795" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ae024b942d2c178c9e2ab625062840cabb3638d8f2be0567152387019ee9a35"} {"question" "e-8875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=y^5-x^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/example-8875.json" "file_sha256" "392637f3a21bc5b0bfc35b6a9ad42912d0fa7422735874bf30b6a44a541de65d"} {"question" "e-8875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(y^5-x^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = u \\, \\mathbf{i} + v \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(v^5-u^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/example-8875.json" "file_sha256" "392637f3a21bc5b0bfc35b6a9ad42912d0fa7422735874bf30b6a44a541de65d"} {"question" "e-8876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\:  \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$x^2z-2y+z=6.$\n What is the third component of $\\mathbf{r}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/example-8876.json" "file_sha256" "d01658de0b773e6dd012fa209d6e891aaeb25d0cc294fad2713b913a80b58b0a"} {"question" "e-8876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}z-2y+z & =6 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)z & =6+2y \\\\ z & =\\frac{2(y+3)}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\left\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\dfrac{2(y+3)}{x^2+1} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\:  {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$ is ${\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/example-8876.json" "file_sha256" "d01658de0b773e6dd012fa209d6e891aaeb25d0cc294fad2713b913a80b58b0a"} {"question" "e-8877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\big\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n${r^2}-4z^2=0$\nfor $z \\geq 0.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/example-8877.json" "file_sha256" "906de1ae25f680d0aac3af81e8e01c48ba2b051050fc8ba30b6aba9f79d76cfe"} {"question" "e-8877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2}-4{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\frac{{r}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{r}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\geq 0.$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle r\\cos\\theta, \\: r\\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z= \\dfrac r2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\left\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: {\\color{black}  \\dfrac r2} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}  \\dfrac r2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/example-8877.json" "file_sha256" "906de1ae25f680d0aac3af81e8e01c48ba2b051050fc8ba30b6aba9f79d76cfe"} {"question" "e-8878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: \\theta \\sin\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi, \\: \\theta \\sin\\phi \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}.$\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/example-8878.json" "file_sha256" "f00ba934461f15bcc4ffbe114768ecb8c17437952510fe4d52da76e779daa39c"} {"question" "e-8878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}\\theta \\cos\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042053/example-8878.json" "file_sha256" "f00ba934461f15bcc4ffbe114768ecb8c17437952510fe4d52da76e779daa39c"}] "usage" {"e-8876" [] "e-8877" [] "q-103796" [] "e-8878" [] "q-103789" [] "q-103787" [] "q-103785" [] "q-103795" [] "e-8875" []} "basis" 1535 "retractions" [[17592186119988 :question/problem "Find a vector-valued function $r(u, v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z = x^{3} - xy$."] [17592186119989 :question/problem "$r(u, v) = ⟨u, v, _____⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u, v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x^{3} + y^{2} - z^{2} = 2$\nfor $z > 0$. What is the third component of $r$?"] [17592186119990 :question/problem "$f(r, θ) = ⟨r\\cos θ, _____, 2r^{2}\\cos θ⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as\n$z = 2r^{2}\\cos θ$.\nWhat is the second component of $f$?"] [17592186119993 :question/problem "$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨ϕ\\cot θ\\sin ϕ, ϕ\\sin ϕ, _____⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$ϕ = ρ\\sin θ$.\nWhat is the third component of $f$?"] [17592186119992 :question/problem "$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨_____, ϕ\\cos θ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ϕ\\cos θ\\cos ϕ⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$ρ = ϕ\\cos θ$.\nWhat is the first component of $f$?"] [17592186119624 :question/problem "Find a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=y^5-x^2$."] [17592186119624 :question/worked-solution "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(y^5-x^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v$, we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = u \\, \\mathbf{i} + v \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(v^5-u^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$"] [17592186119625 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x^2z-2y+z=6$.\nWhat is the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$?"] [17592186119625 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}z - 2y + z &= 6 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 1)z &= 6 + 2y \\\\\nz &= (2(y + 3))/(x^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\left\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\dfrac{2(y+3)}{x^2+1} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v$, we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}}\n$$"] [17592186119626 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\big\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as\n${r^2}-4z^2=0$\nfor $z \\geq 0$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186119626 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form\n\n$$\nz=f(r,\\theta)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} - 4z^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n4z^{2} &= r^{2} \\\\\nz^{2} &= \\frac{r^{2}}{4} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{r}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that\n\n$$\nz\\geq 0\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle r\\cos\\theta, \\: r\\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nz= \\dfrac r2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\left\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: {\\color{black} \\dfrac r2} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black} \\dfrac r2}\n$$"] [17592186119627 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: \\theta \\sin\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi, \\: \\theta \\sin\\phi \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}$.\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186119627 :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\n\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨θ\\cos θ\\sin^{2} ϕ, θ\\sin θ\\sin^{2} ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}\\theta \\cos\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi}$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186116495 17592186116496 17592186116497 17592186116498 17592186116499 17592186116501 17592186116502 17592186116503 17592186116504 17592186116505 17592186116507 17592186116508 17592186116509 17592186116510 17592186116511 17592186116519 17592186116520 17592186116521 17592186116522 17592186116523 17592186116525 17592186116526 17592186116527 17592186116528 17592186116529] "immutable_fields" [[17592186116494 :answer-field/correct] [17592186116494 :answer-field/id] [17592186116494 :answer-field/key] [17592186116494 :answer-field/type] [17592186116500 :answer-field/correct] [17592186116500 :answer-field/id] [17592186116500 :answer-field/key] [17592186116500 :answer-field/type] [17592186116506 :answer-field/correct] [17592186116506 :answer-field/id] [17592186116506 :answer-field/key] [17592186116506 :answer-field/type] [17592186116518 :answer-field/correct] [17592186116518 :answer-field/id] [17592186116518 :answer-field/key] [17592186116518 :answer-field/type] [17592186116524 :answer-field/correct] [17592186116524 :answer-field/id] [17592186116524 :answer-field/key] [17592186116524 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
